# Routh II.Art.150a — the quarter period of the polhode, as an elliptic
# integral — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "The Advanced Part of a Treatise on the Dynamics of a System of
# Rigid Bodies", Part II (Macmillan, 6th ed. 1905), Art. 150a: "The time
# occupied by the instantaneous axis in describing any arc of the polhode has
# already been found in Art. 139. It is there proved that
#
#     lambda*(t - tau) = Integral d(phi) / sqrt(1 - k^2 sin^2 phi)
#
# where the limits are 0 to phi."
#
# THE TWO LANDMARKS. Routh identifies where the two ends of a quarter arc sit:
# at phi = 0 the component w2 vanishes, and the axis is where the polhode cuts
# the plane AOC -- the plane of the GREATEST and LEAST axes. When it reaches
# the plane AOB, w3 = 0 and phi = pi/2. So
#
#     the time from one principal plane to the next is K(k)/lambda
#
# "which is one-fourth of the whole time of revolution", K being the complete
# elliptic integral of the first kind.
#
# THE CONSTANTS, FROM THE TWO QUADRICS. With A > B > C and the invariants
# G^2 and T of Art. 140, the standard reduction of Euler's equations gives
#
#     lambda^2 = (A - B)(G^2 - C*T) / (A*B*C)
#     k^2      = (B - C)(A*T - G^2) / ((A - B)(G^2 - C*T))
#
# Both bracketed differences are non-negative for any real motion, since
# A*T - G^2 = B(A-B)w2^2 + C(A-C)w3^2 and G^2 - C*T = A(A-C)w1^2 + B(B-C)w2^2.
# And k^2 < 1 exactly when G^2 > B*T, which is the case where the polhode
# encircles the greatest axis -- the case set up here.
#
# THE NUMBERS. A cuboid of mass 3 with half-extents (0.5, 1, 2) gives
# I = diag(5, 4.25, 1.25). It is started IN the plane AOC, that is with w2 = 0,
# at w = (2, 0, 1). Then
#
#     G^2 = 101.5625      T = 21.25      G^2 - B*T = +11.25   (encircles A)
#     lambda = 1.4552137502179978
#     k^2    = 0.25   exactly, so k = 1/2
#     K(k)   = 1.685750354812596
#
#     quarter period K/lambda = 1.1584211285524637
#
# and Routh's claim is that the body-frame w3 -- zero nowhere yet -- must vanish
# at precisely that instant, the axis having crossed from plane AOC to plane AOB.
#
# WHAT THE CELLS DO. They run to exactly K/lambda and print the body-frame
# angular velocity. Its z-component is the number to read: it should be zero.
# A second run to 2*K/lambda returns the axis to a plane where w2 vanishes
# instead -- half a revolution of the polhode -- and a third to 4*K/lambda
# completes it and restores the starting state.
#
# SETUP NOTE. system.g_constant = 0. One body only, so gravity would do nothing
# here, but in any multi-body version leaving G = 1 lets the bodies fall
# together into the 1/r^2 singularity and ruins a rotational result for a
# translational reason.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: a box tumbling steadily. Nothing about the picture announces
# the quarter period; it is the elliptic integral that knows.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p2_polhode_quarter_period
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_polhode_quarter_period.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_polhode_quarter_period.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [2, 0, 1] }

get obj0.inertia_tensor
norm(obj0.angular_momentum)
energy

# quarter period: K/lambda = 1.1584211285524637. w3 must vanish here.
run 1.1584211285524637 steps 2
[obj0.orientation.w, 0 - obj0.orientation.x, 0 - obj0.orientation.y, 0 - obj0.orientation.z] * [0, obj0.angular_velocity.x, obj0.angular_velocity.y, obj0.angular_velocity.z] * obj0.orientation

# half period: another quarter, and now w2 vanishes instead.
run 1.1584211285524637 steps 2
[obj0.orientation.w, 0 - obj0.orientation.x, 0 - obj0.orientation.y, 0 - obj0.orientation.z] * [0, obj0.angular_velocity.x, obj0.angular_velocity.y, obj0.angular_velocity.z] * obj0.orientation

# full period: 4*K/lambda from the start -- the axis is home.
run 2.3168422571049274 steps 4
[obj0.orientation.w, 0 - obj0.orientation.x, 0 - obj0.orientation.y, 0 - obj0.orientation.z] * [0, obj0.angular_velocity.x, obj0.angular_velocity.y, obj0.angular_velocity.z] * obj0.orientation
norm(obj0.angular_momentum)
energy
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [2, 0, 1] }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.02
